"Arthur J. O'Dwyer" <ajonospam@andrew.cmu.edu> writes:
> On Thu, 1 Mar 2007, Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

> > wrapping your C code changes in C precompiler code
> > so that it is easy to turn your changes off or on at
> > compile time; for example (ignoring the real code)

>    Bleah! I personally don't think that /every/ patch needs this 
> conditional stuff, since, after all, if some random user decides he 
> doesn't want to use your patch, all he has to do is /not install it/!

Basically so, but eventually you might also have some use for an unpatched
version, and it is easier just to comment out the #defines than to unpatch
the whole thing.
And if you are going to release a patch, the conditionals may make it a
bit easier to combine several patches, and if you release a collection of
various patches, it is easier to see which part is relevant to which patch
(and for the users of a combined diff to turn off individual patches they
don't like).
And generally, like Kent said, to find the patched places in the code.

Basically, I can't see any good reason NOT to use #ifdef's around patched
code.

>    But if you do use #ifdef, please *PLEASE* write
> 
>      #ifdef NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_BOOSTS
>              heal(HowManyPoints, 0);
>      #else
>              heal(HowManyPoints, WhatMaxHpBoost);
>      #endif
> 
> instead of what KPD suggested! Not only is it shorter and more 

Here I totally agree.
(Even though I'm not completely sure whether I have used this convention
in all the patches I've written or maintained myself..)

